---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import ProjectStory from "../../components/ProjectStory.astro";
import { ClientRouter } from "astro:transitions";

const slides = [
  {text: "Once we know the redshifts of galaxies, we can place them in 3D and study how the large-scale structure changes over time. <br> But this video only shows the tip of the iceberg.",},
{text: "Across the whole sky, SDSS has spectra for only a small fraction of all the objects we can see.",},
{text: "Imaging surveys contain many more objects.",},
{text: "The reason is simple. <br> Getting galaxy spectra takes a lot of time and resources. <br> First, we image the sky and find the objects. <br> Then we select which objects to observe. <br> Finally, we take the spectra. <br> It is a long process, but we get very accurate redshifts.",},
{text: "One way to make this faster is to skip spectroscopy and estimate redshifts from images. <br> These are called photometric redshifts, or photo-z. <br> They are much faster, but less accurate.",},
{text: "There is also a third option: clustering redshift. <br> Clustering redshift only needs the positions of galaxies on the sky. <br> From these positions, we can estimate the redshift distribution of a whole galaxy sample. <br> It is fast, and for many studies, it is accurate enough.",},
{text: "The basic idea is simple. <br> We start with a reference sample with known redshifts and a test sample with unknown redshifts. <br> We compare how the two samples cluster on the sky at different redshifts. <br> A strong correlation at a certain redshift tells us that the test sample is likely there. <br> This gives us the bias-weighted redshift distribution.",},
{text: "Since clustering redshift only needs spatial information, we can also apply it to intensity maps.",},
{text: "There are already several public codes for clustering redshift. <br> Most use pair counting.",},
{text: "For every reference object, we find nearby test objects and count them. <br> More pairs mean a stronger correlation.",},
{text: "We repeat this for every reference object. <br> This works well, but it is slow. <br> Users also need to prepare large spectroscopic reference catalogs and deal with systematic effects. <br> These codes are also mainly designed for object catalogs, not intensity maps.",},
{text: "So we developed Tomographer. <br> Tomographer is a clustering-redshift code that makes the process faster and easier. <br> It works for both object catalogs and intensity maps.",},
{text: "The main idea is simple. <br> First, we divide the sky into pixels. <br> We use HEALPix with NSIDE = 2048, giving about 50 million pixels.",},
{text: "For each pixel, we count the reference objects inside our chosen region.",},
{text: "For each pixel, we count the reference objects inside our chosen region.",},
{text: "Doing this across the whole sky gives us a map of reference object counts. <br> We call this the activation map.",},
{text: "Doing this across the whole sky gives us a map of reference object counts. <br> We call this the activation map.",},
{text: "Now we put a new test sample onto the same HEALPix grid.",},
{text: "Instead of finding pairs again, we multiply the activation map by the test map. <br> Then we add up all the pixels. <br> This directly gives us the pair counts.",},
{text: "That's the whole idea. <br> We only do the expensive pair-finding step once. <br> The activation map can then be reused for any number of test samples.",},
{text: "A new sample only needs a simple array multiplication, so the computing time is almost independent of the sample size.",},
{text: "There is another advantage. <br> Both object catalogs and intensity maps become pixel maps. <br> So Tomographer can treat them in exactly the same way.",},
{text: "Using Tomographer is simple. <br> You provide an object catalog or an intensity map and download the precomputed files.",},
{text: "Then you run the code. <br> Tomographer calculates the correlations using fast array operations.",},
{text: "Within minutes, you get the bias-weighted redshift distribution.",},
{text: "Now let me show you how we test the code. <br> First, we use a sample with known spectroscopic redshifts.",},
{text: "The red curve shows the true distribution multiplied by the test-sample bias. <br> Our clustering-redshift measurement agrees very well with the red curve. <br> The normalized residuals follow a Gaussian distribution centered at zero.",},
{text: "Next, we try a harder case with a very sharp redshift distribution.",},
{text: "The edges are easy to blur out. <br> But our measurement still recovers the sharp edges. <br> For comparison, photo-z distributions spread into nearby redshift bins.",},
{text: "Now let's look at a real example: the DESI targets. <br> Before DESI takes spectra, it first images the sky and selects targets from the imaging data.",},
{text: "BGS targets galaxies below redshift 0.4. <br> LRG targets galaxies between redshift 0.4 and 1.1. <br> Using only the imaging catalogs, our clustering-redshift measurements recover these expected ranges.",},
{text: "ELGs are expected to lie between redshift 0.6 and 1.6. <br> QSOs can reach redshifts below about 3. <br> Again, our measurements match the expected ranges very well.",},
{text: "But there is an interesting feature. <br> In the ELG sample, we see extra objects beyond redshift 1.6. <br> This is the highest redshift where the [O II] doublet can still be seen in DESI spectra. <br> A study found that some Lyman-alpha emitters are included in the ELG sample at redshifts above 2. <br> This makes it useful for planning future surveys.",},
{text: "Finally, with Tomographer, we can finish the measurement for the DESI ELG imaging catalog in about 5 minutes.",},
];
---

  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>Yu Voon Ng</title>
    <!-- <ClientRouter /> -->
  </head>


  <body class="project-page">

    <header class="project-nav">
      <a href="/" class="back-link">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M21 12H3M9 6l-6 6 6 6" />
        </svg>
        Yu Voon Ng
      </a>
    </header>


    <main>

      <!-- STORY -->

        <ProjectStory
          title="Tomographer: End-to-end Redshift Distribution Estimation for Source Catalogs and Intensity Maps"
          publication="Chiang, Y.-K., <strong>Ng, Yu Voon</strong>, Lin, Y.-R., Taghizadeh-Popp, M., Ménard, B., 2026, submitted"
          publicationUrl="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03415"
          slides={slides}
          keynote="/presentations/tomo_web/index.html"
          conclusion="Clustering redshift relies only on spatial information to infer the redshift distribution of a galaxy population or an intensity map. To lower the barrier to obtaining clustering-redshift estimates, Tomographer provides an end-to-end framework that reduces the computational complexity from O(NlogN) to O(1). It is applicable to both source catalogs and intensity maps, with further validations and examples presented in the paper."
          conclusionUrl="https://github.com/yuvoonng/tomographer"
        />

    </main>

  </body>
